      "thinking": "The real fault is that io-shop has no timeout on the pricing dependency call, so a slow dependency fully propagates into request latency and causes client disconnects. The docstring admitting \"it waits\" instead of retrying or pricing itself is essentially confessing the flaw \u2014 the code was never designed to survive a degraded dependency.\n\nThe tricky part is that the `ask` callable is synchronous and supplied by the caller, so the shop can't interrupt an in-flight call once it's already blocking \u2014 we can't retroactively time out a call that already took 1500ms to return. A circuit breaker seems like the realistic fix: after detecting repeated slow or failed calls, stop invoking pricing altogether and render a degraded page without the basket total, rather than trying to shorten any individual call.\n\nSince the logs showed all 200 requests slow, a breaker would open quickly. Before committing to that, I want to check if there's an existing timeout/degradation convention elsewhere in the repo \u2014 summary_cache.py already handles cache unreachability gracefully, so reading it might reveal a pattern the pricing service should mirror.\n\n",
